I would split Upgrade V3 into twelve major production departments.

| Department                  | Main objective                             |
| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| 01. Character Studio        | High-quality humanoid characters           |
| 02. Creature Studio         | Monsters, bosses and wildlife              |
| 03. Animation Studio        | Combat, movement and expressions           |
| 04. Rigging Studio          | Reusable, advanced character rigs          |
| 05. Environment Studio      | Regions, buildings and landscapes          |
| 06. Material Studio         | Stylized materials and shaders             |
| 07. Equipment Studio        | Weapons, armour and accessories            |
| 08. Procedural Studio       | Automated asset generation                 |
| 09. VFX Studio              | Magic, combat and environmental effects    |
| 10. Optimization Studio     | Game-ready assets and LODs                 |
| 11. Export Studio           | Automated Unity integration                |
| 12. Asset Management Studio | Asset libraries, validation and versioning |

I would not treat these as twelve unrelated Blender projects.

They should become one connected production ecosystem.

For example, creating a new monster should be able to involve:

* Creature concept and proportions.

* Base mesh generation.

* Sculpting.

* Retopology.

* UV generation.

* Material assignment.

* Rig generation.

* Animation assignment.

* Collision generation.

* LOD generation.

* Export.

* Unity validation.

The fewer repetitive manual steps, the better.

